[
    {
        "content": "<p>While working on some subtraction game theorems and the Spragu-Grundy function, I needed a definition of Mex, which is defined as:</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">mex</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">∉</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{mex}(S) = \\min \\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0} \\mid x \\notin S \\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">mex</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">min</span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mrel mtight\">≥</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">0</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2452em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span></span><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"llap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>This is a kind of trivial definition, but I thought maybe it was already defined and some basic properties proved?</p>",
        "id": 595608186,
        "sender_full_name": "Reuven Peleg",
        "timestamp": 1779020197
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It isn't defined, but it kinda depends what you mean. You're either looking for <code>sInf Sᶜ</code> or <code>WellFounded.min Sᶜ</code>, and prefer the first one if you can. We do have lemmas about both of these expressions.</p>\n<p>cc <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"459227\">@Violeta Hernández</span></p>",
        "id": 595610674,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1779021580
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>We used to have <code>Ordinal.mex</code> defined for <code>Ordinal</code>, but we now simply use <code>sInf sᶜ</code> to avoid unnecessary API duplication.</p>",
        "id": 595610866,
        "sender_full_name": "Yuyang Zhao",
        "timestamp": 1779021707
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/16874\">#16874</a> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/16989\">#16989</a> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35669\">#35669</a> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/35769\">#35769</a> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36539\">#36539</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/vihdzp/combinatorial-games/blob/950ee6c735d1778f252fce14e9d1c88ac910aa9a/CombinatorialGames/Game/Impartial/Grundy.lean#L41-L42\">this from CGT</a></p>",
        "id": 595611137,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1779021893
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Also CGT already has the Sprague-Grundy function IIUC, is it possible that you're working on something that already exists?</p>",
        "id": 595611347,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1779022046
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There is something a little counterintuitive about taking the complement of a <del>collection</del> set of ordinals. (Likewise cardinals, I assume.) The type theory is sound but it <em>feels</em> scary...</p>",
        "id": 595611625,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1779022221
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In case you didn't know, combinatorial games are now developed in a separate repository, since mathlib lacks reviewers for this area: <a href=\"https://github.com/vihdzp/combinatorial-games\">https://github.com/vihdzp/combinatorial-games</a>. You can find the Sprague-Grundy theorem at <a href=\"https://vihdzp.github.io/combinatorial-games/CombinatorialGames/Game/Impartial/Grundy.html#IGame.Impartial.nim_grundy_equiv\">https://vihdzp.github.io/combinatorial-games/CombinatorialGames/Game/Impartial/Grundy.html#IGame.Impartial.nim_grundy_equiv</a>.</p>",
        "id": 595611844,
        "sender_full_name": "Yuyang Zhao",
        "timestamp": 1779022365
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks a lot for the references, those are super helpful!</p>",
        "id": 595639503,
        "sender_full_name": "Reuven Peleg",
        "timestamp": 1779037675
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1060393\">Chris C</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Minimal.20Excluded.20Function.20.28mex.29/near/595611625\">dijo</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>There is something a little counterintuitive about taking the complement of a <del>collection</del> set of ordinals. (Likewise cardinals, I assume.) The type theory is sound but it <em>feels</em> scary...</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The distinction between sets and classes that you're used to is exercised a bit differently in Lean. A \"set\" is really a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Small#doc\">docs#Small</a> type, and a \"class\" is one that isn't. So e.g. we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ordinal.bddAbove_of_small#doc\">docs#Ordinal.bddAbove_of_small</a>, telling you that a small set of ordinals is always bounded. We also have a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ConditionallyCompleteLinearOrder#doc\">docs#ConditionallyCompleteLinearOrder</a> instance on ordinals, telling you that any bounded non-empty set has a supremum, as you might expect.</p>",
        "id": 599246186,
        "sender_full_name": "Violeta Hernández",
        "timestamp": 1780344628
    }
]